<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Makahiki</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Makahiki was the ancient Hawaiian New Year festival in honour of the god Lono, lasting four lunar months from about October or November to February or March; on Hawaiʻi island it began with the crescent moon after the Pleiades, Makaliʻi, rose shortly after sunset. Work and war were forbidden; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konohiki">konohiki</a> collected offerings of pigs, taro, kapa and other goods for Lono's altars, while priests carried his image, the Akua Loa, around each island. Sports, hula and feasting followed, and at the end a canoe loaded with offerings was set adrift for Lono. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a> during Makahiki in 1778.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancient Hawaiians divided the year into two seasons: Makahiki, and eight lunar months in which the rituals of the god Kū were practised. In the Hawaiian language makahiki means year, and also the change from the harvest season to the planting season. The word probably comes from Makaliʻi hiki, the rising of the Pleiades, though it may also come from ma Kahiki, roughly as in Tahiti, since the legend of Lono is tied to voyages to and from Tahiti. The festival's origins are linked to the return of Lono in the form of a mortal man during an early migration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> the season might begin when the Pleiades rose above Puʻu o Mahuka Heiau as seen from Kaena Point, or when the star ʻAʻa, Sirius, appeared above a particular cliff. Hawaiians gave thanks to Lono-ika-makahiki, who brought life, peace and victory, prayed for the death of their enemies, and commoners prayed that their chiefs' lands would grow. Since there was no money, offerings were also left at ahu, stone altars on the boundaries of each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahupua-a">ahupuaʻa</a>. The king placed a niho palaoa necklace on the Akua Loa, which was adorned with white kapa streamers.</p><p>The contests included boxing, hōlua sledding on lava courses, foot racing, wrestling, javelin throwing, surfing and canoe races, alongside games of the mind: riddles, proverbs and the recitation of genealogies. A contestant's showing reflected on the family, and people bet on their favourites. When the chief came back to shore at the festival's end, warriors threw spears at him, and he had to parry them to prove himself worthy to rule. A royal child born during the season was sometimes named Lono i ka makahiki.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The sails and masts of Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>'s ship resembled the Akua Loa, and his vessel anchored in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kealakekua-bay">Kealakekua Bay</a> near a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">heiau</a> to Lono. The Keauhou Holua National Historic Landmark preserves one of the best-kept lava sled courses. The traditional games are still played to teach younger generations about their ancestors, and the Aloha Festivals, first held as Aloha Week, celebrate the Makahiki tradition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Makahiki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lono was worshipped during the <b>Makahiki</b> season and Kū in times of war.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hawaiian-religion">Hawaiian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of each <b>Makahiki</b> season warriors hurled spears at him as he came ashore, and he had to parry them to prove himself worthy to rule.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>Priests (kahuna) of the Hawaiian religion recited the Kumulipo during the <b>makahiki</b> season, which honoured the god Lono.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a></li></ul></section>
